definition but mention the operators. Actually, to be thorough and avoid underselling, let me count both ways or pick the literal definition and be consistent.\n\nHmm, I realize the cleanest approach: follow the literal definition given (conditionals = if/switch/?:). Count operators as part of expressions (not separately massed). This is consistent and the delta still shows improvement.\n\nLet me also reconsider: are `&&`, `||` \"conditionals\"? In some formulations yes. Let me actually count them as conditionals to be more inclusive, because short-circuit logical operators ARE control flow (they branch). `===` is a comparison (boolean-valued). Let me decide on a consistent rule:\n\n**My APP counting rule (stated up front):**\n- Constants: numeric/string/boolean literals.\n- Bindings: variable/parameter references and declarations (names).\n- Invocations: function/method calls including `new`.\n- Conditionals: `if`, `switch`, `?:`, and short-circuit logical operators `&&`, `||` (they control flow), and `!`. Comparisons `===`, `!==` I'll treat as... hmm. Actually, let me treat `===`/`!==`/`<`/`>` as comparisons = conditionals too, since they produce booleans used in control flow. This is more inclusive.\n\nUgh, this is getting complicated. Let me just pick the LITERAL definition from the prompt to avoid debate: conditionals = if/switch/?:. I'll note that logical/comparison operators are expression operators not separately counted. This is the cleanest, most defensible reading of THE GIVEN spec.\n\nOK let me re-tally with literal APP (conditionals = if/switch/?: only; logical/comparison/arithmetic operators not massed):\n\nLet me re-tally the whole `nextGeneration` current:\n\n```\nconst live = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y)));\n```\n- Bindings: cells, live, x, y = 4\n- Invocations: new Set, cells.map, cellKey = 3\n- Loops: map = 1\n- Assignments: const live = 1\n= 4 + 6 + 5 + 6 = 21\n\n```\nconst liveNeighbors = (x, y) => NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS.filter(([dx,dy]) => live.has(cellKey(x+dx, y+dy))).length;\n```\n- Bindings: liveNeighbors, x, y, dx, dy = 5\n- Invocations: filter, live.has, cellKey = 3\n- Loops: filter = 1\n- Assignments: const liveNeighbors = 1\n= 5 + 6 + 5 + 6 = 22\n\n```\nconst next = new Map<string, Cell>();\n```\n- Bindings: next = 1\n- Invocations: new Map = 1\n- Assignments: 1\n= 1 + 2 + 6 = 9\n\n```\nconst evaluateCell = (x, y) => { ... }\n```\n- Bindings: evaluateCell, x, y = 3\n- Assignments: 1\n= 3 + 6 = 9\n\n```\nconst count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n```\n- Bindings: count = 1\n- Invocations: liveNeighbors = 1\n- Assignments: 1\n= 1 + 2 + 6 = 9\n\n```\nconst alive = live.has(cellKey(x, y));\n```\n- Bindings: alive = 1\n- Invocations: live.has, cellKey = 2\n- Assignments: 1\n= 1 + 4 + 6 = 11\n\n```\nconst survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\n```\n- Bindings: survives, alive, count = 3\n- Constants: 2, 3 = 2\n- Assignments: 1\n= 3 + 2 + 6 = 11\n\n```\nconst born = !alive && count === 3;\n```\n- Bindings: born, alive, count = 3\n- Constants: 3 = 1\n- Assignments: 1\n= 3 + 1 + 6 = 10\n\n```\nif (survives || born) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\n- Bindings: survives, born, x, y = 4 (x, y in cellKey call and [x,y] array)\n- Constants: none ([x,y] contains bindings x,y, not literals)\n- Invocations: next.set, cellKey = 2\n- Conditionals: if = 1\n- Assignments: 0\n= 4 + 4 + 4 = 12\n\n```\nfor (const [x, y] of cells) {\n  evaluateCell(x, y);\n  for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n    evaluateCell(x + dx, y + dy);\n  }\n}\n```\n- Bindings: x, y, dx, dy = 4 (cells is already counted as param; NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS is module binding)\n- Invocations: evaluateCell (x2) = 2\n- Loops: for (outer), for (inner) = 2\n- Assignments: const [x,y] = 1, const [dx,dy] = 1 = 2\n= 4 + 4 + 10 + 12 = 30\n\n```\nreturn [...next.values()];\n```\n- Bindings: next = 1\n- Invocations: next.values = 1, spread/Array construction = 1 (the `[...]`)\n- Assignments: 0\n= 1 + 4 = 5\n\nNow sum for nextGeneration: 21+22+9+9+9+11+11+10+12+30+5 = 149\n\nPlus cellKey (9) + NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS (22) = 31\n\nTotal current mass ≈ 149 + 31 = 180.\n\nHmm wait, let me re-add nextGeneration: 21+22=43; +9=52; +9=61; +9=70; +11=81; +11=92; +10=102; +12=114; +30=144; +5=149. Yes 149. Plus 31 = 180.\n\n### Refactored code mass\n\nRefactored `nextGeneration` (rename `live`→`liveCells`, `next`→`nextCells`, simplify decision):\n\n```\nconst liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y)));\n```\nSame as before (just renamed live→liveCells, same count): 21\n\n```\nconst liveNeighbors = (x, y) => NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS.filter(([dx,dy]) => liveCells.has(cellKey(x+dx, y+dy))).length;\n```\nSame: 22\n\n```\nconst nextCells = new Map<string, Cell>();\n```\nSame: 9\n\n```\nconst evaluateCell = (x, y) => { ... }\n```\nSame: 9\n\n```\nconst count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n```\nSame: 9\n\n```\nconst alive = liveCells.has(cellKey(x, y));\n```\nSame: 11\n\n```\n// comment\nif (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {\n  nextCells.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\n- Bindings: count, alive, x, y = 4\n- Constants: 3, 2 = 2\n- Invocations: nextCells.set, cellKey = 2\n- Conditionals: if = 1\n= 4 + 2 + 4 + 4 = 14\n\nWait, I removed `survives` (11) and `born` (10) lines, and replaced the `if (survives || born) {...}` (12) with `if (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {...}` (14).\n\nOriginal subtotal for decision region: 11 (survives) + 10 (born) + 12 (if) = 33.\nNew subtotal: 14 (if with inline condition).\nSavings: 33 - 14 = 19.\n\nSo new nextGeneration total: 149 - 33 + 14 = 130.\n\nNew file total: 130 + 31 = 149. (was 180)\n\nMass change: 180 → 149, delta -31. \n\nHmm, but wait — I should double check I'm being fair. The `survives` and `born` bindings referenced `alive` and `count`. In the new version, the `if` references `count`, `alive` directly. So those bindings (alive, count) are still bound earlier. I counted them in the new `if` block (count, alive = part of the 4 bindings). And in the original, I counted alive & count within the survives/born lines. So no double counting issue — in original, alive/count were counted in survives and born lines (and also in the `alive =` and `count =` lines). Wait, that means in the original I counted `alive` and `count` references multiple times (once where declared, once in survives, once in born). Is that right? APP counts each occurrence of a binding as mass 1. So yes, each usage of a variable is a binding reference (mass 1). So in original:\n- `count` appears in: `const count =` (1, as declaration/binding), `survives` line (count, count = 2 refs), `born` line (count = 1 ref). \n\nHmm wait, declaration vs reference. In APP, a binding is \"Variables, parameters\". Each occurrence? Or each distinct variable? The formula `(bindings x 1)` — typically counts each binding occurrence (each time a variable name appears). Let me assume each occurrence counts. So `count` appearing 3 times = 3 binding-mass.\n\nIn my tally:\n- `const count = liveNeighbors(x, y)`: count declared = 1 binding.\n- `const survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3)`: count ×2 = 2 bindings; alive ×1.\n- `const born = !alive && count === 3`: count ×1, alive ×1.\n- New `if (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2))`: count ×2, alive ×1.\n\nIn original, count occurrences: 1 (decl) + 2 (survives) + 1 (born) = 4. alive: 1 (decl) + 1 (survives) + 1 (born) = 3.\nIn new, count occurrences: 1 (decl) + 2 (if) = 3. alive: 1 (decl) + 1 (if) = 2.\n\nSo new saves: count 1 occurrence, alive 1 occurrence = 2 binding mass, plus removing survives & born declarations (2 bindings + 2 assignments = 2 + 12 = 14), plus the constants (2, 3 in survives; 3 in born = 3 constants; new has 3, 2 = 2 constants; saves 1 constant).\n\nLet me recount the decision region carefully:\n\nORIGINAL decision region:\n- `const survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);`\n  - bindings: survives(1), alive(1), count(2) = 4\n  - constants: 2, 3 = 2\n  - assignments: 1\n  - = 4 + 2 + 6 = 12\n- `const born = !alive && count === 3;`\n  - bindings: born(1), alive(1), count(1) = 3\n  - constants: 3 = 1\n  - assignments: 1\n  - = 3 + 1 + 6 = 10\n- `if (survives || born) { next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]); }`\n  - bindings: survives(1), born(1), x(1), y(1) [in cellKey and array] ... wait x appears in cellKey(x,y) and [x,y] = 2; y appears in cellKey(x,y) and [x,y] = 2. So x=2, y=2. survives=1, born=1. = 6 bindings.\n  \n  Hmm, I need to be careful. Let me recount: `next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y])`:\n  - x appears in `cellKey(x, y)` and in `[x, y]` → 2 occurrences\n  - y appears in `cellKey(x, y)` and in `[x, y]` → 2 occurrences\n  - next = 1\n  So bindings in the if-body: next(1) + x(2) + y(2) = 5. Plus condition `survives || born`: survives(1), born(1) = 2. Total bindings = 7.\n  - invocations: next.set, cellKey = 2\n  - conditionals: if = 1\n  - = 7 + 4 + 4 = 15\n\nHmm, earlier I said 12 for the if. Let me redo. I think I undercounted. Let me recount original decision region:\n- survives line: 12\n- born line: 10\n- if line: 15\nTotal original decision = 37.\n\nNEW decision region:\n- `if (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) { nextCells.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]); }`\n  - Condition: count(2), alive(1) = 3 bindings; constants 3, 2 = 2\n  - Body: nextCells(1), x(2), y(2) = 5 bindings; invocations nextCells.set, cellKey = 2\n  - conditionals: if = 1\n  - bindings total: 3 + 5 = 8\n  - = 8 + 2(constants) + 4(invocations) + 4(conditional) = 18\n\nWait, that doesn't seem to save as much. Let me recompute. Original decision = 37, new = 18. Savings = 19. OK that matches my earlier ~19 estimate. Good.\n\nHold on, let me recheck the original if-line count. `if (survives || born) { next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]); }`:\n- condition bindings: survives, born = 2\n- body bindings: next, x (×2), y (×2) = 5\n- total bindings = 7\n- invocations: next.set, cellKey = 2 → 4\n- conditionals: if = 1 → 4\n- constants: 0\n- = 7 + 4 + 4 = 15. Yes.\n\nAnd survives line: `const survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);`\n- bindings: survives, alive, count, count = 4\n- constants: 2, 3 = 2\n- assignments: 1 → 6\n- = 4 + 2 + 6 = 12. Yes.\n\nborn line: `const born = !alive && count === 3;`\n- bindings: born, alive, count = 3\n- constants: 3 = 1\n- assignments: 1 → 6\n- = 3 + 1 + 6 = 10. Yes.\n\nOriginal decision total = 12 + 10 + 15 = 37.\n\nNew if line: `if (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) { nextCells.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]); }`\n- condition bindings: count, count, alive = 3\n- condition constants: 3, 2 = 2\n- body bindings: nextCells, x, x, y, y = 5\n- invocations: nextCells.set, cellKey = 2 → 4\n- conditionals: if = 1 → 4\n- = (3+5) + 2 + 4 + 4 = 8 + 2 + 4 + 4 = 18. Yes.\n\nNew decision total = 18.\n\nSavings = 37 - 18 = 19.\n\nSo nextGeneration mass: original 149 (let me re-verify with corrected if=15 not 12).\n\nLet me re-sum original nextGeneration with corrected numbers:\n- const live line: 21\n- liveNeighbors: 22\n- const next: 9\n- evaluateCell decl: 9\n- const count: 9\n- const alive: 11\n- survives line: 12\n- born line: 10\n- if line: 15\n- nested for loops: 30\n- return: 5\nSum = 21+22+9+9+9+11+12+10+15+30+5 = 153. \n\n(Previously I had 149 because I used if=12; correcting to 15 gives 153.)\n\nNew nextGeneration:\n- const liveCells: 21 (same)\n- liveNeighbors: 22 (same, just renamed liveCells)\n- const nextCells: 9 (same)\n- evaluateCell decl: 9 (same)\n- const count: 9 (same)\n- const alive: 11 (same, renamed liveCells)\n- if line (combined): 18\n- nested for: 30 (same, renamed nextCells inside? no, the for loops call evaluateCell, no next ref) = 30\n- return: 5 (nextCells instead of next, same count) = 5\nSum = 21+22+9+9+9+11+18+30+5 = 134.\n\nFile total:\n- Original: cellKey(9) + NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS(22) + nextGeneration(153) = 184\n- New: cellKey(9) + NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS(22) + nextGeneration(134) = 165\n\nMass change: 184 → 165, delta -19.\n\nGreat, that's a solid, defensible mass reduction of 19, achieved by eliminating two intermediate bindings (`survives`, `born`) and folding the survival/birth rule into a single direct expression.\n\nNow, is this a Rule 2 (reveals intent) win or a Rule 4 (fewest elements) win? It's primarily Rule 4 (fewer elements) — we removed two bindings and two assignments. But with a clear comment, it also maintains Rule 2. The comment \"A cell is alive next generation iff it has exactly 3 live neighbors (birth or survival) or is alive with exactly 2 (survival)\" makes the intent explicit.\n\nI'm fairly confident the combined form with the comment is at least as clear as the original with named booleans, and it's more concise. Let me also keep the naming improvements minimal — actually, let me NOT rename `live`→`liveCells` and `next`→`nextCells` to keep this a single focused change (one improvement: simplify the decision logic). Hmm, but the renames are arguably part of clarity. \n\nDecision: To respect \"ONE improvement at a time,\" I'll make the focused improvement = **simplify the survival/birth decision into a single direct expression of the Game of Life rule** (Rule 4 + Rule 2 via explanatory comment). I'll leave the names `live`/`next` as-is since they're adequate and renaming is a separate concern. Actually, `live` and `next` are a bit terse... but they're fine and used consistently. Keeping them keeps the diff minimal and the refactoring focused.\n\nHmm, but actually — let me reconsider. The instruction also strongly emphasizes \"Naming is first priority.\" Let me at least evaluate naming and decide. \n\n`live` — a Set<string> of live cell keys. Used as `live.has(...)`. It's acceptable; \"live\" as a noun phrase (\"the live [cells]\") reads okay. `liveCells` would be marginally clearer but `live` is fine.\n`next` — Map<string,Cell> of next-gen cells. `next` is generic. `nextCells` clearer. But `next` reads fine with context.\n`evaluateCell` — evaluates whether cell is alive next gen. Could be `decideCell` or `processCell`. `evaluateCell` is oka