ors(x, y);\nconst alive = live.has(key);\n...\nnext.set(key, [x, y]);\n```\nThis reduces redundant invocations (Rule 4, fewer elements / and minor DRY). But it adds a binding. Mass trade-off: removes 1 invocation (2 mass), adds 1 binding (1 mass) and 1 assignment (6 mass). Wait that increases mass. Hmm, unless I count it as removing 1 invocation = -2, adding 1 binding +1 and 1 assignment +6 = net +5. So that's worse by mass. But it's arguably slightly clearer and avoids recomputation. Not a clear win.\n\nOK here's my plan. Let me do a focused refactoring that:\n1. Improves naming: `live` → `liveCells`, `next` → `nextCells` (Rule 2)\n2. Simplifies the survival/birth logic into a single clear expression (Rule 4, and arguably Rule 2 with a good comment)\n\nActually, let me reconsider #2. The risk is that combining `survives || born` into `count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)` might be seen as less intent-revealing because it loses the \"survives\"/\"born\" concepts. But Game of Life's rules can be stated very cleanly as: \"a cell is alive next generation iff it has 3 live neighbors, or it is alive and has 2 live neighbors.\" That IS the canonical formulation. So expressing it directly is arguably MORE intent-revealing.\n\nLet me go with the combined approach but keep it clear. Actually, I want to be careful here — the task says \"make ONE improvement at a time\" and \"Tests must stay green\". Let me do a clean, safe, well-justified refactoring.\n\nLet me decide on the refactoring. I'll do:\n\n**Refactoring: Combine the survival/birth decision into a single direct expression of the Game of Life rule, and improve naming of the accumulator sets.**\n\nWait, the guidance says \"Make ONE improvement at a time\" and \"Never refactor multiple things at once\". Let me focus on the single most impactful improvement.\n\nI think the best single improvement is **simplifying the survival/birth logic**. The current code:\n```typescript\nconst survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\nconst born = !alive && count === 3;\nif (survives || born) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nThe two intermediate boolean bindings `survives` and `born`, combined with `survives || born`, can be simplified. Note:\n- `survives` = `alive && (count === 2 || count === 3)`\n- `born` = `!alive && count === 3`\n- `survives || born` = `alive && (count === 2 || count === 3) || !alive && count === 3`\n\nSince `alive && count === 3` and `!alive && count === 3` are mutually exclusive and cover all cases of `count === 3`:\n= `(count === 3) || (alive && count === 2)`\n\nSo:\n```typescript\n// A cell is alive next generation iff it has exactly 3 live neighbors\n// (birth or survival) or is alive with exactly 2 live neighbors (survival).\nconst aliveNext = count === 3 || (alive && count === 2);\nif (aliveNext) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nThis:\n- Removes 1 binding (`born`)\n- Removes 1 `!` operation\n- Removes 1 `||` \n- Simplifies the condition\n\nActually let me recount. Original:\n```\nconst survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);   // binding survives, invocations: none (operators), conditionals: && and (|| ) \nconst born = !alive && count === 3;                         // binding born, conditional: &&, !\nif (survives || born) {                                     // conditional: ||\n```\n\nNew:\n```\nconst aliveNext = count === 3 || (alive && count === 2);   // binding aliveNext, conditional: || and &&\nif (aliveNext) {\n```\n\nSo the new version has fewer conditionals and one fewer binding. This reduces mass.\n\nHmm wait, but is `aliveNext` needed as a binding? I could inline it:\n```typescript\nif (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nInlining removes the binding and assignment but the conditional stays in the `if`. Let me think about clarity. With a comment explaining the rule, inlining is clean:\n```typescript\n// Game of Life rule: a cell is alive next generation iff it has exactly 3\n// live neighbors (birth or survival) or is currently alive with exactly 2\n// (survival).\nif (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but the named variables `survives`/`born` were intent-revealing. By inlining, I rely on the comment. That's a Rule 2 vs Rule 4 trade-off. The comment makes the intent clear, and the code is more concise.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest, most defensible approach that clearly improves things: keep the named variables because they reveal intent (Rule 2 > Rule 4), but recognize that the naming could be slightly better. Hmm.\n\nLet me step back. What's the GENUINELY best improvement here that I can defend?\n\nOption A: Naming improvements only (`live` → `liveCells`, `next` → `nextCells`). Safe, Rule 2, no mass change really (same number of bindings).\n\nOption B: Simplify the boolean logic. Reduces mass (Rule 4), arguably maintains intent with a comment.\n\nOption C: Both.\n\nThe instructions say \"Make ONE improvement at a time.\" But also the refactor subagent should \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring.\" Let me do a coherent single refactoring. I'll combine naming + logic simplification as one cohesive \"clean up the decision logic and naming\" pass, since they're in the same region. Actually, to be safe and follow \"one improvement at a time,\" let me focus.\n\nI think the most valuable single improvement is the **logic simplification** because it reduces mass AND simplifies the code. But I want to keep it intent-revealing. Let me go with the combined approach using a clear comment.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether the combined form is truly clearer. The Game of Life rules:\n1. Any live cell with fewer than two live neighbours dies (underpopulation).\n2. Any live cell with two or three live neighbours lives on.\n3. Any live cell with more than three live neighbours dies (overpopulation).\n4. Any dead cell with exactly three live neighbours becomes a