rovement lies elsewhere — the survival/birth decision logic.\n\n## Step 2: Current APP Mass (focus on the decision region in `evaluateCell`)\n\nThe current decision logic:\n```typescript\nconst survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\nconst born = !alive && count === 3;\nif (survives || born) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\nThis introduces two intermediate bindings (`survives`, `born`) that are each used exactly once, then combined. The Game of Life \"alive next generation\" rule has a canonical positive form: **a cell is alive next gen iff it has exactly 3 live neighbors (birth or survival) or is alive with exactly 2 (survival).**\n\nAPP counting (conditionals = `if`/`switch`/`?:`; logical/comparison operators are expression operators):\n\n**Original decision region:**\n- `survives` line: bindings 4 (survives, alive, count×2) + constants 2 (2,3) + assignment 1 = 12\n- `born` line: bindings 3 (born, alive, count) + constants 1 (3) + assignment 1 = 10\n- `if` line: bindings 7 (survives, born, next, x×2, y×2) + invocations 2 + conditional 1 = 15\n- **Subtotal: 37**\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n- **Rule 1 (Tests pass)**: All 9 green — safe to refactor.\n- **Rule 2 (Reveals intent)**: The two named booleans map to rule terminology, but they're single-use intermediates. A single direct expression + an explanatory comment states the canonical rule more concisely without losing clarity.\n- **Rule 3 (DRY)**: `cellKey(x, y)` is recomputed in `evaluateCell` (in `live.has` and `next.set`). Binding it once would save an invocation but *adds* a binding+assignment (net +5 mass) — not a clear win; skip.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest elements)**: Folding `survives || born` into `count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)` removes 2 bindings + 2 assignments. Clear win.\n\n**Refactoring chosen**: Simplify the survival/birth decision into one direct expression of the rule (Rule 4, with a comment preserving Rule 2).\n\n## Step 4: Implement"},{"type":"toolCall","