ined with `survives || born`, can be simplified. Note:\n- `survives` = `alive && (count === 2 || count === 3)`\n- `born` = `!alive && count === 3`\n- `survives || born` = `alive && (count === 2 || count === 3) || !alive && count === 3`\n\nSince `alive && count === 3` and `!alive && count === 3` are mutually exclusive and cover all cases of `count === 3`:\n= `(count === 3) || (alive && count === 2)`\n\nSo:\n```typescript\n// A cell is alive next generation iff it has exactly 3 live neighbors\n// (birth or survival) or is alive with exactly 2 live neighbors (survival).\nconst aliveNext = count === 3 || (alive && count === 2);\nif (aliveNext) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nThis:\n- Removes 1 binding (`born`)\n- Removes 1 `!` operation\n- Removes 1 `||` \n- Simplifies the condition\n\nActually let me recount. Original:\n```\nconst survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);   // binding survives, invocations: none (operators), conditionals: && and (|| ) \nconst born = !alive && count === 3;                         // binding born, conditional: &&, !\nif (survives || born) {                                     // conditional: ||\n```\n\nNew:\n```\nconst aliveNext = count === 3 || (alive && count === 2);   // binding aliveNext, conditional: || and &&\nif (aliveNext) {\n```\n\nSo the new version has fewer conditionals and one fewer binding. This reduces mass.\n\nHmm wait, but is `aliveNext` needed as a binding? I could inline it:\n```typescript\nif (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nInlining removes the binding and assignment but the conditional stays in the `if`. Let me think about clarity. With a comment explaining the rule, inlining is clean:\n```typescript\n// Game of Life rule: a cell is alive next generation iff it has exactly 3\n// live neighbors (birth or survival) or is currently alive with exactly 2\n// (survival).\nif (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y),