o: `(alive && (count === 2 || count === 3)) || (!alive && count === 3)`\n\nSimplified: `count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)` — because count===3 covers both \"alive with 3\" and \"dead with 3\".\n\nThis is correct and is the standard concise form. I'm confident in it. And I verified with all tests.\n\nLet me also double check: is there any case where `count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)` differs from the original?\n\nOriginal: `(alive && (count === 2 || count === 3)) || (!alive && count === 3)`\n- alive=T, count=2: original T. New: count===3(F) || (T && T) = T. ✓\n- alive=T, count=3: original T. New: T || ... = T. ✓\n- alive=T, count=4: original F. New: F || (T && F) = F. ✓\n- alive=T, count=1: original F. New: F || (T && F) = F. ✓\n- alive=F, count=3: original T. New: T || ... = T. ✓\n- alive=F, count=2: original F. New: F || (F && T) = F. ✓\n- alive=F, count=4: original F. New: F || (F && F) = F. ✓\n\nAll match. \n\nNow let me write the refactored version. I'll keep the named variable for clarity but combine the logic:\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y)));\n\n  const liveNeighbors = (x: number, y: number): number =>\n    NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS.filter(([dx, dy]) =>\n      liveCells.has(cellKey(x + dx, y + dy)),\n    ).length;\n\n  const nextCells = new Map<string, Cell>();\n  const evaluateCell = (x: number, y: number): void => {\n    const count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n    const alive = liveCells.has(cellKey(x, y));\n    // A cell is alive next generation iff it has exactly 3 live neighbors\n    // (birth or survival) or is alive with exactly 2 (survival).\n    if (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {\n      nextCells.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n    }\n  };\n\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    evaluateCell(x, y);\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      evaluateCell(x + dx, y + dy);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return [...nextCells.values()];\n}\n```\n\n