ey(x, y), [x, y]);\n    }\n```\n\nThis is the focused change. Let me apply it.\n\nLet me make sure the oldText is unique. The block with `const survives` and `const born` is unique. Good.\n\nLet me apply the edit, then run tests.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the comment wording to be precise. \"reproduction or survival\" for count===3: a dead cell with 3 neighbors is born (reproduction); a live cell with 3 neighbors survives. \"alive with exactly 2 (survival)\": a live cell with 2 neighbors survives. Good. The comment is accurate.\n\nLet me apply.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 9 tests pass. Now let me evaluate the implementation for refactoring.\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation\n\n- `nextGeneration` — clearly reveals intent (compute next generation). ✓\n- `cellKey`, `NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS`, `liveNeighbors`, `evaluateCell`, `count`, `alive` — all clear and match their purpose.\n- `live` (Set of live cell keys), `next` (Map of next-gen cells) — terse but adequate and used consistently.\n\n**Decision**: Names adequately reveal intent. The clearest improvement lies elsewhere — the survival/birth decision logic.\n\n## Step 2: Current APP Mass (focus on the decision region in `evaluateCell`)\n\nThe current decision logic:\n```typescript\nconst survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\nconst born = !alive && count === 3;\nif (survives || born) {\n  next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n}\n```\nThis introduces two intermediate bindings (`survives`, `born`) that are each used exactly once, then combined. The Game of Life \"alive next generation\" rule has a canonical positive form: **a cell is alive next gen iff it has exactly 3 live neighbors (birth or survival) or is alive with exactly 2 (survival).**\n\nAPP counting (conditionals = `if`/`switch`/`?:`; logical/comparison operators are expression operators):\n\n**Original decision region:**\n- `survives` line: bindings 4 (survives, alive, count×2) + constants 2 (2,3) + assignment 1 = 12\n- `born` line: bindings 3 (born, alive, count) + constants 1 (3) + assignment 1 = 10\n- `if` line: bindings 7 (survives, born, next, x×2, y×2) + invocations 2 + conditional 1 = 15\n- **Subtotal: 37**\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n- **Rule 1 (Tests pass)**: All 9 green — safe to refactor.\n- **Rule 2 (Reveals intent)**: The two named booleans map to rule terminology, but they're single-use intermediates. A single direct expression + an explanatory comment states the canonical rule more concisely without losing clarity.\n- **Rule 3 (DRY)**: `cellKey(x, y)` is recomputed in `evaluateCell` (in `live.has` and `next.set`). Binding it once would save an invocation but *adds* a binding+assignment (net +5 mass) — not a clear win; skip.\n- **Rule 4 (Fewest elements)**: Folding `survives || born` into `count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)` removes 2 bindings + 2 assignments. Clear win.\n\n**Refactoring chosen**: Simplify the survival/birth decision into one direct expression of the rule (Rule 4, with a comment preserving Rule 2).\n\n## Step 4: Implement"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0e43b104c1dd47799d8569de","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"    const count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n    const alive = live.has(cellKey(x, y));\n    // A cell is alive next generation iff it has exactly 3 live neighbors\n    // (reproduction or survival) or is alive with exactly 2 (survival).\n    if (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {\n      next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n    }","oldText":"    const count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n    const alive = live.has(cellKey(x, y));\n    const survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\n    const born = !alive && count === 3;\n    if (survives || born) {\n      next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n    }"}],"path":"src/game-of-life.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":254,"output":13233,"cacheRead":8000,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":12361,"totalTokens":2148