iginal nextGeneration with corrected numbers:\n- const live line: 21\n- liveNeighbors: 22\n- const next: 9\n- evaluateCell decl: 9\n- const count: 9\n- const alive: 11\n- survives line: 12\n- born line: 10\n- if line: 15\n- nested for loops: 30\n- return: 5\nSum = 21+22+9+9+9+11+12+10+15+30+5 = 153. \n\n(Previously I had 149 because I used if=12; correcting to 15 gives 153.)\n\nNew nextGeneration:\n- const liveCells: 21 (same)\n- liveNeighbors: 22 (same, just renamed liveCells)\n- const nextCells: 9 (same)\n- evaluateCell decl: 9 (same)\n- const count: 9 (same)\n- const alive: 11 (same, renamed liveCells)\n- if line (combined): 18\n- nested for: 30 (same, renamed nextCells inside? no, the for loops call evaluateCell, no next ref) = 30\n- return: 5 (nextCells instead of next, same count) = 5\nSum = 21+22+9+9+9+11+18+30+5 = 134.\n\nFile total:\n- Original: cellKey(9) + NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS(22) + nextGeneration(153) = 184\n- New: cellKey(9) + NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS(22) + nextGeneration(134) = 165\n\nMass change: 184 → 165, delta -19.\n\nGreat, that's a solid, defensible mass reduction of 19, achieved by eliminating two intermediate bindings (`survives`, `born`) and folding the survival/birth rule into a single direct expression.\n\nNow, is this a Rule 2 (reveals intent) win or a Rule 4 (fewest elements) win? It's primarily Rule 4 (fewer elements) — we removed two bindings and two assignments. But with a clear comment, it also maintains Rule 2. The comment \"A cell is alive next generation iff it has exactly 3 live neighbors (birth or survival) or is alive with exactly 2 (survival)\" makes the intent explicit.\n\nI'm fairly confident the combined form with the comment is at least as clear as the original with named booleans, and it's more concise. Let me also keep the naming improvements minimal — actually, let me NOT rename `live`→`liveCells` and `next`→`nextCells` to keep this a single focused change (one improvement: simplify the decision logic). Hmm, but the renames are arguably part of clarity. \n\nDecision: To respect \"ONE improvement at a time,\" I'll make the focused improvement = **simplify the survival/birth decision into a single direct expression of the Game of Life rule** (Rule 4 + Rule 2 via explanatory comment). I'll leave the names `live`/`next` as-is since they're adequate and renaming is a separate concern. Actually, `live` and `next` are a bit terse... but they're fine and used consistently. Keeping them keeps the diff minimal and the refactoring focused.\n\nHmm, but actually — let me reconsider. The instruction also strongly emphasizes \"Naming is first priority.\" Let me at least evaluate naming and decide. \n\n`live` — a Set<string> of live cell keys. Used as `live.has(...)`. It's acceptable; \"live\" as a noun phrase (\"the live [cells]\") reads okay. `liveCells` would be marginally clearer but `live` is fine.\n`next` — Map<string,Cell> of next-gen cells. `next` is generic. `nextCells` clearer. But `next` reads fine with context.\n`evaluateCell` — evaluates whether cell is alive next gen. Could be `decideCell` or `processCell`. `evaluateCell` is okay.\n\nThese are all \"okay.\" The naming is adequate. The clearest win is the logic simplification. Let me go with that as the single improvement, and document that naming was evaluated and kept.\n\nLet me now write the refactored code. I'll use `edit` to make the precise change.\n\nThe change:\n```typescript\n    const count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n    const alive = live.has(cellKey(x, y));\n    const survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\n    const born = !alive && count === 3;\n    if (survives || born) {\n      next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n    }\n```\n→\n```typescript\n    const count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n    const alive = live.has(cellKey(x, y));\n    // A cell is alive next generation iff it has exactly 3 live neighbors\n    // (reproduction or survival) or is alive with exactly 2 (survival).\n    if (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {\n    