h` is NOT a conditional; it's a binding access + comparison. The `&&` isn't listed either, so it'd be... an invocation-like operator?\n\nHmm, the APP categories are ambiguous for operators. Let me assume `===` and `&&` are not counted as conditionals (only `if`/`switch`/`?:` are). Then:\n\nCurrent `sameCells`:\n- Constants: `false` (1) = 1\n- Bindings: `a`, `b`, `a.length`, `b.length`, `bKeys`, `cell`, `b`, `key`, `cell`... let me count occurrences: `a`(2: param + .length), `b`(2: param + .length + map = 3 actually), `bKeys`(2: assign + .has), `cell`(2: param + key arg)... this is getting tedious.\n- Invocations: `.map(key)`, `new Set(...)`, `.every(...)`, `key(cell)`, `bKeys.has(...)` = 5 invocations = 10\n- Conditionals: 1 `if` = 4\n- Assignments: `const bKeys =` = 6\n- Loops: `map` (1) + `every` (1) = 10\n\nTotal roughly: 1 + ~8 + 10 + 4 + 6 + 10 = ~39\n\nProposed (no if):\n```typescript\nconst sameCells = (a: Cell[], b: Cell[]): boolean => {\n  const bKeys = new Set(b.map(key));\n  return a.length === b.length && a.every((cell) => bKeys.has(key(cell)));\n};\n```\n- Constants: 0 (no `false`)\n- Bindings: similar ~8\n- Invocations: `.map`, `new Set`, `.every`, `key`, `.has` = 10\n- Conditionals: 0 (no if; the `===` and `&&` aren't `if`/`switch`/`?:`)\n- Assignments: `const bKeys` = 6\n- Loops: `map` + `every` = 10\n\nTotal: ~8 + 10 + 0 + 6 + 10 = ~34\n\nSo removing the `if` and merging into a boolean expression reduces mass by ~5 (the `if` conditional at 4 + the `false` constant at 1) and removes an element (the early-return branch). This is a legitimate Rule 4 (fewer elements) AND APP improvement!\n\nBut does it hurt Rule 2 (clarity)? The original early-return `if (a.length !== b.length) return false;` is a very clear fast-fail. The merged version `a.length === b.length && a.every(...)` is also clear: \"same length AND every cell matches.\" Both are readable. The merged version is slightly more concise. I'd argue clarity is preserved (Rule 2 not hurt).\n\nBut there's a subtle behavioral/performance consideration: the original short-circuits on length mismatch BEFORE building the Set (`new Set(b.map(key))`). The merged version builds the Set first, THEN checks length. For a length mismatch, the merged version does wasted work building the Set. But for correctness, no difference. For our tiny inputs, performance is irrelevant. And `&&` short-circuits so `a.every` isn't run on length mismatch. The only \"waste\" is building the Set, which is cheap.\n\nHmm, but the early-return also makes the \"different lengths → not equal\" intent very explicit. The merged form is fine though.\n\nActually, this is a reasonable refactoring: it reduces mass (removes a conditional + constant) and reduces elements (removes a branch), while keeping clarity. This satisfies Rule 4 and APP, doesn't hurt Rule 2.\n\nBut wait — is removing the early return actually better? The early return is a common, clear pattern. Some would argue it's MORE readable (fail fast). The merged single-expression return is also a recognized clean pattern. It's a judgment call. Given the refactor mandate and that this genuinely reduces mass + elements without hurting clarity, I think it's defensible.\n\nHmm, but I want to be careful not to make a change that's debatable in clarity. Let me think about which a reviewer would prefer.\n\n`sameCells` original:\n```typescript\nif (a.length !== b.length) return false;\nconst bKeys = new Set(b.map(key));\nreturn a.every((cell) => bKeys.has(key(cell)));\n```\nvs\n```typescript\nconst bKeys = new Set(b.map(key));\nreturn a.length === b.length && a.every((cell) => bKeys.has(key(cell)));\n```\n\nI actually find the original slightly clearer because the length guard is visually prominent and the \"happy path\" reads top-to-bottom. The merged version packs more into one line.\n\nGiven Rule 2 > Rule 4 > APP, and the original is arguably clearer, I should NOT make this change if it hurts clarity even slightly. Hmm.\n\nOK, let me step back 