retty clean and readable. \n\nLet me focus on a targeted, safe refactoring:\n\n1. Rename `consider` to a clearer name.\n2. Compute `cellKey(x, y)` once per `consider` call to reduce redundant invocations.\n\nLet me think about whether renaming `consider` improves clarity. The function:\n- Takes a cell position\n- Counts its live neighbors\n- Determines if it survives or is born\n- Adds it to the next generation if so\n\n`advanceCell` - \"advances\" the cell to next gen. Decent.\n`evaluateCell` - evaluates the cell's fate. Decent.  \n`populateNext` - populates the next gen with this cell if alive. Hmm.\n`decideFate` - decides the fate of the cell.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest name that reveals intent is something like `advanceCell` or `nextGenCell`. But these still need to communicate that it conditionally adds to `next`.\n\nHmm, actually maybe the cleanest refactoring is to make `consider` return a boolean or the cell, rather than mutating `next` as a side effect. But that changes structure significantly.\n\nLet me reconsider. The key insight: the current code computes `cellKey(x, y)` redundantly. In `consider`:\n- `liveNeighbors(x, y)` computes `cellKey(x+dx, y+dy)` for each of 8 neighbors — that's necessary, different keys.\n- `live.has(cellKey(x, y))` — the cell's own key.\n- `next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y])` — the cell's own key again.\n\nSo the cell's own key is computed twice. We could store it:\n\n```typescript\nconst consider = (x: number, y: number): void => {\n  const key = cellKey(x, y);\n  const count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n  const alive = live.has(key);\n  const survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\n  const born = !alive && count === 3;\n  if (survives || born) {\n    next.set(key, [x, y]);\n  }\n};\n```\n\nThis reduces redundant `cellKey` invocations from 2 to 1 per consider call. That's a small mass improvement (removes 1 invocation = 2 mass per consider call, but consider is called up to 9N times).\n\nHmm, but actually the reduce in `liveNeighbors` creates a closure each call. Let me see if there's a cleaner formulation.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. Let me count the current mass carefully and think about a cleaner version.\n\nLet me reconsider what's the highest-value refactoring here.\n\n**Option A: Rename `consider` + compute key once**\n\nThis improves intent (Rule 2) and slightly reduces mass (Rule 4).\n\n**Option B: Restructure to count-based approach**\n\nThis is a bigger change. Let me think about whether it's cleaner.\n\nActually, let me look at the `liveNeighbors` function more carefully:\n\n```typescript\nconst liveNeighbors = (x: number, y: number): number =>\n  NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS.reduce(\n    (count, [dx, dy]) => count + (live.has(cellKey(x + dx, y + dy)) ? 1 : 0),\n    0,\n  );\n```\n\nThis is a reduce with a ternary. Mass-wise:\n- Invocation: reduce (1) = 2\n- Loop: reduce = 5\n- Bindings: count, dx, dy = 3\n- Conditional: ternary = 4\n- Invocations: live.has (per iteration, but counted once in source) = 2, cellKey (per iteration) = 2\n- Constants: 0, 1 = 2\n- Operators: + (count + ...), arithmetic x+dx, y+dy — these aren't counted in APP typically\n\nThis is reasonable. \n\nLet me think about the overall structure. I think the cleanest improvement is:\n\n1. Rename `consider` → `advanceCell` (reveals intent better)\n2. Hoist the cell's own key computation to avoid redundancy\n\nLet me also reconsider: is `next` a good name? It's a Map of next-gen cells. `nextGeneration` would clash with the function name. `nextCells` or `survivors` or `result`. `next` is OK but a touch vague. Maybe `nextGen` or `born`. Hmm. Actually since the function is `nextGeneration` and returns cells, calling the accumulator `next` is fine and conventional.\n\nLet me also reconsider `live`. `live` is a Set of live cell keys. Good name.\n\nOK here's my plan. Let me make a focused refactoring:\n\n1. Rename `consider` to `advanceCell` — reveals that it advances a single cell to the next generation.\n2. Compute the cell's own key once and reuse it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the naming more carefully. \"advanceCell\" - does it advance? It determines the cell's state in the next generation and records it. \"advance\" implies moving forward, which fits. But it might also imply it returns the advanced cell.\n\nAlternatively, the cleanest semantic name: the function's job is to \"add the cell to the next generation if it should be alive\". So `addIfAliveNext` or `includeIfAliveNext`. But these are verbose.\n\nHow about keeping the structure but making `consider` a clearly-named helper. Let me go with `evaluateCell` — it evaluates whether the cell is alive next generation and records it.\n\nHmm, actually I realize there may be a cleaner overall approach. Let me think about the standard, elegant Game of Life implementation.\n\nThe cleanest functional approach:\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const isAlive = (x: number, y: number): boolean =>\n    live.has(`${x},${y}`);\n  const countLiveNeighbors = (x: number, y: number): number =>\n    NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS.filter(([dx, dy]) => isAlive(x + dx, y + dy)).length;\n\n  const live = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => `${x},${y}`));\n  // candidate cells: all live cells + their neighbors\n  const candidates = new Set<string>();\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    candidates.add(`${x},${y}`);\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      candidates.add(`${x + dx},${y + dy}`);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const result: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const key of candidates) {\n    const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n    const count = countLiveNeighbors(x, y);\n    if (count === 3 || (count === 2 && isAlive(x, y))) {\n      result.push([x, y]);\n    }\n  }\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nThis introduces string parsing (`key.split(\",\").map(Number)`) which is a bit ugly and adds mass. Not clearly better.\n\nLet me reconsider. The current implementation is actually quite good. It avoids parsing by keeping `[x, y]` tuples in the `next` Map keyed by string.\n\nI think the highest-value, lowest-risk refactoring is:\n1. Rename `consider` → clearer name\n2. Dedupe the `cellKey(x, y)` computation\n\nLet me also double check: is there duplication between \"survives\" and \"born\"? \n- `survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3)`\n- `born = !alive && count === 3`\n- `if (survives || born)`\n\nThe combined condition: a cell is alive next gen if `count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)`. This is the classic rule. We could simplify:\n\n```typescript\nconst alive = live.has(key);\nconst count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\nconst aliveNext = count === 3 || (alive && count === 2);\nif (aliveNext) {\n  next.set(key, [x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nThis replaces `survives` + `born` (2 bindings, 2 conditionals, && and ||) with a single `aliveNext` binding and a combined conditional. Let me evaluate mass.\n\nOriginal:\n```typescript\nconst alive = live.has(key);\nconst survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\nconst born = !alive && count === 3;\nif (survives || born) {\n  next.set(key, [x, y]);\n}\n```\n- Bindings: alive, survives, born = 3\n- Constants: 2, 3, 2, 3 = 4\n- Conditionals: `alive && (count===2 || count===3)` has `||` (1) + `&&`(1) ... and `!alive && count===3` has `&&`(1). Plus the `if` (1). So conditionals: `count===2`, `count===3` are comparisons (counted as conditionals? The rule says \"Control flow (if, switch, ?:)\". Comparisons `===` aren't explicitly control flow. Hmm. Let me treat `===` as part of conditional expressions. Actually ternary `?:` is listed but `===` is a comparison operator. Let me count logical operators and if as conditionals.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me simplify my approach: I'll count the major elements.\n\nOriginal `consider` body:\n- `const key = ...` (if I add the dedup) \n- `const count = liveNeighbors(x, y)` — invocation (liveNeighbors) + binding (count) + assignment\n- `const alive = live.has(key)` — invocation (live.has) + binding (alive) + assignment\n- `const survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3)` — conditional (&&), conditional (||), conditional(===2), conditional(===3), bindings 2,3 constants, assignment\n- `const born = !alive && count === 3` — conditional(!), conditional(&&), conditional(===3), constant 3, assignment\n- `if (survives || born)` — conditional (||) + if\n- `next.set(key, [x, y])` — invocation + [x,y] array (bindings x,y)\n\nThis is a lot. The simplified version:\n```typescript\nconst key = cellKey(x, y);\nconst count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\nconst alive = live.has(key);\nif (count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)) {\n  next.set(key, [x, y]);\n}\n```\n- Invocation: cellKey (1) = 2\n- Bindings: key, count, alive, x, y = 5 (x,y are params)\n- Constants: 3, 2 = 2\n- Conditionals: `count===3` (1), `alive && count===2` (&& = 1, ===2 = 1), `||` (1), `if` (1) = 5\n- Invocations: liveNeighbors (1) = 2, live.has (1) = 2, next.set (1) = 2\n- Assignments: key, count, alive = 3 (×6 = 18)\n\nHmm. vs original with dedup added:\n```typescript\nconst key = cellKey(x, y);\nconst count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\nconst alive = live.has(key);\nconst survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\nconst born = !alive && count === 3;\nif (survives || born) {\n  next.set(key, [x, y]);\n}\n```\n- Bindings: key, count, alive, survives, born, x, y = 7\n- Constants: 2, 3, 3 = 3 (the 2,3 in survives and 3 in born)... wait survives has 2 and 3, born has 3. So constants: 2, 3, 3 = 3\n- Conditionals: survives: &&, ||, ===2, ===3 (4); born: !, &&, ===3 (3); if + || (2) = 9\n- Invocations: cellKey(1), liveNeighbors(1), live.has(1), next.set(1) = 4 → 8\n- Assignments: key, count, alive, survives, born = 5 → 30\n\nSimplified version:\n- Bindings: key, count, alive, x, y = 5\n- Constants: 3, 2 = 2\n- Conditionals: count===3, alive&&count===2 (&&, ===2), ||, if = 5\n- Invocations: cellKey, liveNeighbors, live.has, next.set = 4 → 8\n- Assignments: key, count, alive = 3 → 18\n\nSo simplified reduces: bindings 7→5 (−2), constants 3→2 (−1), conditionals 9→5 (−4×... wait conditionals are ×4). \n\nMass delta:\n- Bindings: −2 × 1 = −2\n- Constants: −1 × 1 = −1\n- Conditionals: −4 × 4 = −16\n- Assignments: −2 × 6 = −12\n\nTotal delta ≈ −31 mass. That's a meaningful reduction AND it's clearer (the survival rule is stated in its canonical form: alive next iff 3 neighbors, or 2 neighbors and currently alive).\n\nBut wait — does simplifying hurt clarity? The original explicitly names `survives` and `born`, which maps to the Game of Life rules (survival and birth). That's actually quite clear and reveals intent. The simplified `count === 3 || (alive && count === 2)` is the canonical compact form but loses the named \"survival\" and \"birth\" concepts.\n\nHmm, this is a Rule 2 vs Rule 4 tension. The named version is arguably more intent-revealing (survival vs birth are distinct concepts in GoL). The compact version is fewer elements but denser.\n\nPer the rules: Rule 2 (Reveals Intent) trumps Rule 4 (Fewest Elements). So if `survives`/`born` better reveal intent, I should keep them.\n\nBut are `survives` and `born` really clearer than the canonical rule? The canonical rule \"a cell is alive next generation if it has exactly 3 live neighbors, or if it's currently alive and has exactly 2\" is THE Game of Life rule. Most GoL readers recognize it instantly. The `survives`/`born` split is also recognizable.\n\nI think this is a judgment call. Let me consider: the `survives`/`born` naming explicitly separates the two cases (survival and reproduction), which are the two ways a cell becomes/stays alive. This is intent-revealing. I'll lean towards keeping the named version but maybe simplifying the `if` to `if (survives || born)` → already that.\n\nActually, the current code already has `survives` and `born`. So the \"simplification\" to one condition would reduce intent. Let me NOT do that, per Rule 2.\n\nSo my refactoring should focus on:\n1. Naming: rename `consider` to something clearer.\n2. Dedup `cellKey(x, y)`.\n\nLet me reconsider the name `consider`. The function \"considers\" a cell — evaluates whether it should be alive in the next generation and adds it to the result if so. \n\nBetter names:\n- `evaluateCell` — evaluates the cell's fate. Clear.\n- `advanceCell` — advances the cell to next gen.\n- `placeIfAlive` — places the cell in next if it'll be alive.\n\nHmm, `advanceCell` is nice and short. But does it \"advance\"? It computes the next state. I think `evaluateCell` is clearest about \"look at this cell and decide its fate.\"\n\nActually, you know what, let me reconsider. The function both evaluates AND records (mutates `next`). A pure name like `evaluateCell` hides the side effect. \n\nMaybe the cleanest is to restructure so `consider` returns the cell-or-null, and the caller filters. But that's a bigger change.\n\nLet me reconsider. Actually I think a clean refactoring is to have the function return whether the cell is alive next, and let the loop add it:\n\n```typescript\nconst isAliveNext = (x: number, y: number): boolean => {\n  const count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n  const alive = live.has(cellKey(x, y));\n  return count === 3 || (alive && count === 2);\n};\n\nconst next = new Map<string, Cell>();\nfor (const [x, y] of cells) {\n  if (isAliveNext(x, y)) next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n  for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n    const nx = x + dx, ny = y + dy;\n    if (isAliveNext(nx, ny)) next.set(cellKey(nx, ny), [nx, ny]);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nBut this loses the `survives`/`born` distinction and re-evaluates. Also `isAliveNext` is called for the live cell itself AND potentially multiple times for shared neighbors. The original `consider` with a Set/Map dedupes via `next.set` (last write wins, same value). With `isAliveNext` returning a bool, we'd call it redundantly but `next.set` dedupes the key. That's fine semantically but does redundant computation.\n\nHmm, the original calls `consider` for each live cell + 8 neighbors = up to 9N calls, and `next.set` dedupes. So redundant computation already happens (a neighbor shared by 2 live cells gets `consider` called twice). The original accepts this. So `isAliveNext` approach has same redundancy. Fine.\n\nBut this restructure changes the structure quite a bit and the `survives`/`born` clarity. Let me NOT do the big restructure.\n\nLet me go with the minimal, safe, intent-improving refactoring:\n\n1. Rename `consider` → `evaluateCell`.\n2. Compute `cellKey(x, y)` once per call and reuse.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether `evaluateCell` is clearly better than `consider`. \n\n`consider` — to think about, take into account. In context \"consider a cell\" = take this cell into account for the next generation. It's a bit informal/vague.\n\n`evaluateCell` — assess/evaluate the cell. Slightly more precise: it's evaluating the cell's fate.\n\nI think `evaluateCell` is marginally better. But is it worth the churn? The naming evaluation says: \"Does this name clearly describe what the function actually does?\" `consider` is vague. `evaluateCell` is clearer. Let me rename.\n\nHmm, but actually, maybe a more descriptive name given it records the result: `recordCellFate` or `applyRules`. \n\nLet me go with `evaluateCell` — it's clear that we're evaluating the cell against the rules, and the side effect (recording in `next`) is acceptable for an inner helper. Actually, to be more honest about the side effect, maybe `addToNextIfAlive`? That's verbose but accurate.\n\nI'll go with `evaluateCell`. Hmm, wait. Let me reconsider once more. \n\nActually I realize the cleanest thing might be to inline `consider` since it's called in two places but those places are adjacent (the live cell and its neighbors). But inlining would duplicate the survival logic. Not good.\n\nOK, decision: rename `consider` → `evaluateCell`, and dedupe `cellKey(x, y)`.\n\nWait, let me reconsider the dedup. Currently:\n```typescript\nconst consider = (x: number, y: number): void => {\n  const count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n  const alive = live.has(cellKey(x, y));\n  const survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\n  const born = !alive && count === 3;\n  if (survives || born) {\n    next.set(cellKey(x, y), [x, y]);\n  }\n};\n```\n\n`cellKey(x, y)` appears twice (in `alive` and in `next.set`). Dedup:\n```typescript\nconst evaluateCell = (x: number, y: number): void => {\n  const key = cellKey(x, y);\n  const count = liveNeighbors(x, y);\n  const alive = live.has(key);\n  const survives = alive && (count === 2 || count === 3);\n  const born = !alive && count === 3;\n  if (survives || born) {\n    next.set(key, [x, y]);\n  }\n};\n```\n\nThis adds 1 binding (key) + 1 assignment but removes 1 invocation (cellKey) — net: +1 (binding) +6 (assignment) −2 (invocation) = +5 mass. Hmm, that INCREASES mass by 5!\n\nWait, that's because assignments are expensive (×6). Adding a `const key = cellKey(x,y)` costs 1 binding + 1 assignment + 1 invocation = 1+6+2 = 9, and we remove 1 invocation (the second cellKey call) = 2. Net +7. That's worse for mass!\n\nBut it reduces redundant computation (calls cellKey once instead of twice). Mass-wise it's worse, but performance-wise better. Per APP, lower mass is better, so deduping the key INCREASES mass. Interesting.\n\nHmm. So per APP, the dedup is a net negative. But it removes redundant work. The rules say Rule 4 (Fewest Elements) is lowest priority, and APP is a guideline. But adding a binding+assignment to remove an invocation... \n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Is `cellKey(x, y)` called twice really \"duplication\" in the DRY sense? It's the same expression `cellKey(x, y)` with the same args. DRY says \"knowledge should have single representation.\" The knowledge \"the key for cell (x,y)\" is computed twice. Computing it once and storing is the DRY way. But it adds an assignment.\n\nRule 3 (DRY) vs Rule 4 (Fewest Elements): Rule 3 is higher priority. So removing the duplic